<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ouro Preto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vila Rica, Vila Rica de Ouro Preto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ouro Preto, formerly Vila Rica, is a town in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, founded during the gold rush of the late 17th and early 18th centuries. It was the capital of the Captaincy of Minas Gerais and the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inconfidencia-mineira">Inconfidência Mineira</a> conspiracy of 1789. Its baroque churches, including works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleijadinho">Aleijadinho</a>, made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ouro-preto.jpg" alt="Ouro Preto" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raquel Mendes Silva · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conjunto_arquitet%C3%B4nico_e_urban%C3%ADstico_de_Ouro_Preto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1695 Manuel Borba Gato found gold at Rio das Velhas, the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-gold-rush">Brazilian Gold Rush</a>, and a gold-bearing stream was found at Itacolomi between 1695 and 1696. Vila Rica, meaning Rich Town, was established in 1698, and in 1711 several small settlements were united as a municipality. Vila Rica became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> from 1720 and was the focal point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s golden age under Portuguese rule. Officially 800 tons of gold were sent to Portugal in the 18th century, not counting what moved illegally or stayed in the colony, as in the gold used to decorate churches.</p><p>The wealth drew the intelligentsia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the baroque revival known as the Barroco Mineiro flourished, seen in the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleijadinho">Aleijadinho</a>, the painter Manoel da Costa Ataíde, the composer Lobo de Mesquita and the poet Tomás António Gonzaga. It became one of the most populous cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, about 40,000 people in 1730 and 80,000 in 1750, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> had fewer than 8,000.</p><p>In 1789 the town was the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inconfidencia-mineira">Inconfidência Mineira</a>, a failed attempt to gain independence from Portugal. Its leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiradentes">Joaquim José da Silva Xavier</a>, known as Tiradentes, was hanged as a warning to future revolutionaries. On 20 May 1823 the village was raised to a city and took the name Ouro Preto, meaning Black Gold, from the gold covered with a layer of iron oxide found there. The Escola de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> was created in 1876. In 1897 the state government moved to the new planned city of Belo Horizonte, because the needs of government had outgrown the town in its valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ouro Preto lies in the Serra do Espinhaço mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and its centre keeps well-preserved Portuguese colonial architecture with few signs of modern development. Its 18th- and 19th-century churches are decorated with gold and with the sculptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleijadinho">Aleijadinho</a>, and it was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> colonial architecture. The Museu da Inconfidência occupies the former municipal palace on Tiradentes Square. The Universidade Federal de Ouro Preto has about 10,000 students, many living in communal houses called repúblicas, a tradition shared with the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ouro Preto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the end of the seventeenth century the bandeirantes found gold in Minas Gerais, and in the first half of the eighteenth century a rush of people from the colony and from Portugal built <b>Vila Rica de Ouro Preto</b>, Sabará and Mariana from 1711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After a tax revolt at <b>Vila Rica</b> in 1720 led by Filipe dos Santos, the crown separated the mining region from the Captaincy of São Paulo as its own captaincy, with Vila Rica, now Ouro Preto, as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Captaincy of Minas Gerais</a></li></ul></section>
